    "knowledge_point": "Solving a Quadratic Equation When the Solutions Are Irrational",
    "problem": "The equation ${z}^{2}-25=-10z$ can be written in the form $(z+p{)}^{2}=q.$ Find $p+q.$",
    "worked_solution": "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, the $-10z$ term to the left-hand side, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{2}-25 & =-10z \\\\ {z}^{2}+10z & =25 \\\\ {z}^{2}+2\\cdot 5\\cdot z & =25 \\\\ {z}^{2}+2\\cdot 5\\cdot z+{5}^{2}-{5}^{2} & =25 \\\\ (z+5{)}^{2}-{5}^{2} & =25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we isolate the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(z+5{)}^{2}-{5}^{2} & =25 \\\\ (z+5{)}^{2} & =50\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $p=5,q=50,$ and $p+q=55.$",
